Subject: [PATCH] webgl: serve claimed extensions that need no driver support

getSupportedExtensions() and getExtension() include five extensions when
the profile claims them, whatever the driver supports: EXT_render_snorm,
EXT_texture_norm16, WEBGL_blend_func_extended,
WEBGL_compressed_texture_pvrtc and WEBGL_render_shared_exponent. Each
exposes only constants, so getExtension() returns a real object and a
page that lists and hashes extensions sees the claimed GPU's set.
Extensions with methods stay off unless the driver supports them, since
calling one would fail. Extensions the profile does not claim are
removed.

--- a/third_party/blink/renderer/modules/webgl/webgl_rendering_context_base.cc
+++ b/third_party/blink/renderer/modules/webgl/webgl_rendering_context_base.cc
@@ -25,7 +25,9 @@
 
 #include "third_party/blink/renderer/modules/webgl/webgl_rendering_context_base.h"
 
+#include <array>
 #include <set>
+#include <string_view>
 
 #include "base/apostate/profile.h"
 
@@ -3839,8 +3841,74 @@
   if (tracker->Developer() &&
       !RuntimeEnabledFeatures::WebGLDeveloperExtensionsEnabled())
     return false;
-  if (!tracker->Supported(this))
-    return false;
+
+  // WebGL extension names a profile may claim on a host whose GL stack does
+  // not provide them.
+  //
+  // Short and by name on purpose. Every entry exposes constants, internal
+  // formats or blend factors and NO methods, so the object getExtension()
+  // returns is a complete object: a page enumerates it, reads its constants
+  // and hashes the list, and all of that succeeds. What does not succeed is a
+  // page that renders THROUGH one -- an R16 texture on a stack without
+  // GL_EXT_texture_norm16 raises GL_INVALID_ENUM -- and that residual is
+  // narrower than the name being absent, which every detector reads.
+  //
+  // Deliberately absent, each for its own reason:
+  //
+  //   OVR_multiview2                   framebufferTextureMultiviewOVR() is a
+  //                                    method; a page told the extension
+  //                                    exists and then calling it gets an
+  //                                    error out of a call that was promised.
+  //   WEBGL_provoking_vertex           the same, provokingVertexWEBGL().
+  //   EXT_disjoint_timer_query_webgl2  methods, and worse than useless here: a
+  //                                    working GPU timer on a software
+  //                                    rasteriser hands a page the throughput
+  //                                    ratio directly, which is the one GPU
+  //                                    contradiction no string can cover.
+  //   KHR_parallel_shader_compile      no methods, but COMPLETION_STATUS_KHR
+  //                                    is read through getProgramParameter and
+  //                                    a page that polls it would never see a
+  //                                    program finish. Unnecessary anyway on
+  //                                    the deployment target: patch 0052
+  //                                    enables it natively on Linux
+  //                                    SwiftShader.
+  static constexpr std::array<std::string_view, 5> kServableWithoutDriver = {
+      "EXT_render_snorm", "EXT_texture_norm16", "WEBGL_blend_func_extended",
+      "WEBGL_compressed_texture_pvrtc", "WEBGL_render_shared_exponent"};
+
+  // The profile's extension list, spelled as getSupportedExtensions() spells
+  // the names. An empty list means the profile does not describe its
+  // extensions, so neither half of this applies.
+  const base::apostate::Profile* profile = base::apostate::Profile::Get();
+  const std::set<std::string>* claimed =
+      profile && !profile->gl_extensions().empty() ? &profile->gl_extensions()
+                                                   : nullptr;
+  const std::string_view name(tracker->ExtensionName());
+  const bool profile_claims_it =
+      claimed && claimed->find(std::string(name)) != claimed->end();
+
+  if (!tracker->Supported(this)) {
+    // The addition direction, which patch 0028 left closed because it judged
+    // every addition unsafe. That was too strong. Blink carries a complete
+    // implementation class for each name above and the only thing refusing is
+    // the driver-support lookup in Supported(), so serving one yields a real
+    // object rather than a promise nothing backs -- measured on this host,
+    // where ANGLE/Metal returns an object for all of them and
+    // ANGLE/SwiftShader returns null for five.
+    //
+    // Open only for the names above, and only when the profile asks. The
+    // failure mode to avoid is not over-claiming, it is self-contradiction: a
+    // name in getSupportedExtensions() whose getExtension() returns null is a
+    // stronger tell than the name being absent, because no device does that.
+    // Both paths reach this function, so the two cannot disagree.
+    bool servable_without_driver = false;
+    for (const std::string_view servable : kServableWithoutDriver) {
+      servable_without_driver = servable_without_driver || servable == name;
+    }
+    if (!profile_claims_it || !servable_without_driver) {
+      return false;
+    }
+  }
   if (disabled_extensions_.Contains(String(tracker->ExtensionName())))
     return false;
 
@@ -3851,18 +3919,12 @@
   // D3D11 machine never exposes. The extension list is hashed by fingerprinting
   // scripts, so three extra entries make the hash match no real Windows Chrome.
   //
-  // This is the removal direction only, and that is the whole point. Adding an
-  // extension is unsafe because a page calls getExtension() and then calls
-  // methods on the object; removal cannot fail, because the page is told no
-  // exactly as the real device would tell it. Serving here rather than at
-  // getSupportedExtensions() covers both, since getExtension() reaches the same
-  // check — a list and an object that disagreed would be worse than either.
-  if (const base::apostate::Profile* profile = base::apostate::Profile::Get()) {
-    const std::set<std::string>& allowed = profile->gl_extensions();
-    if (!allowed.empty() &&
-        allowed.find(std::string(tracker->ExtensionName())) == allowed.end()) {
-      return false;
-    }
+  // Removal has no failure mode at all: the page is told no exactly as the real
+  // device would tell it. Serving here rather than at getSupportedExtensions()
+  // covers both, since getExtension() reaches the same check — a list and an
+  // object that disagreed would be worse than either.
+  if (claimed && !profile_claims_it) {
+    return false;
   }
   return true;
 }
